 ## Overview
-Direct Light Contribution Estimation computes the intensity of light arriving directly from a specific light source at a point in a participating medium, accounting for attenuation by both local geometry and global scene occlusion. It solves the problem of accurately modeling first-order scattering (in-scattering) from direct illumination in volume rendering, where naive global opacity interpolation fails to capture fine local occlusion details. It is distinguished from its siblings by explicitly separating the attenuation calculation into a high-resolution local segment and a lower-resolution global segment, whereas [Light Sampling for NEE](2616-light-sampling-for-nee.md) focuses on unbiased Monte Carlo integration for indirect paths, and [Direct Illumination Sampling with Mesh Intersection](4450-direct-illumination-sampling-with-mesh-intersection.md) handles opaque geometry intersections rather than volumetric attenuation.
